# The 200 brightest stars in the near infrared (2MASS Ks band, 2.16 um), excluding the Sun.
#
# Columns: (formal name, common name or None, RA [deg], Dec [deg], spectral type, Ks mag)
#
# Magnitudes: 2MASS All-Sky Point Source Catalog (Skrutskie+ 2006, CDS II/246), Ks.
#   Every star here saturates the 2MASS detectors; its Ks comes from fitting the
#   unsaturated PSF wings, with typical errors of 0.15-0.35 mag (not ~0.02 as for fainter
#   stars). Most of these stars are also variable (many are Miras with K amplitudes of
#   ~1 mag), and 2MASS is a single epoch (1997-2001). Treat the ranking as approximate.
#   Alpha Centauri A+B is unresolved in 2MASS and appears once, under Alpha1 Centauri,
#   with combined light.
# The cutoff falls within a tie at Ks = -0.12.
#
# Positions: ICRS / J2000.0 epoch and equinox. For the 190 stars matched (within 6") to
#   Hipparcos: from the HYG v4.1 database, which takes them from the Hipparcos catalog
#   (typical accuracy ~1 mas). For the 10 stars with no Hipparcos entry (IK Tau, RW Vel,
#   V PsA, RR Car, W Aql, NS Vul, HD 53917, SU Vel, MN Vel, RS Vel): 2MASS positions
#   (~0.1", at the 2MASS observation epoch). Given here to 1e-6 deg (0.0036").
# Spectral types: Yale Bright Star Catalog, 5th ed. (fallback: Hipparcos via HYG; for
#   non-Hipparcos stars, GCVS, else Skiff's Catalogue of Stellar Spectral Classifications,
#   CDS B/mk). Some fallback types are crude or doubtful (e.g. 'M...', and 'M8V' for the
#   M giant V450 Aql).
# Formal names: Bayer designation, else Flamsteed, else HR (Bright Star) number, else
#   GCVS variable-star designation, else HD number.
# Common names: IAU-approved proper names as listed in HYG v4.1.
#
# Sources: HYG database (CC BY-SA 4.0), github.com/astronexus/HYG-Database;
#   Yale BSC5 (CDS V/50); 2MASS PSC, GCVS (B/gcvs) and B/mk via VizieR.

